"""Additional original teaching passages. Apply after the course authoring scripts."""
import json,pathlib
ROOT=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
passages={
'c2-u01':('Use a paradigm to solve a conversation problem', '''Imagine that a colleague asks, **¿A qué hora vuelven ustedes?** You plan to return at six. Begin with **Volvemos a las seis**, because your answer changes the perspective from “you all” to “we.” The stem therefore returns to **volv-**. Copying *vuelven* from the question would keep the wrong subject. If you answer only for yourself, choose **Vuelvo a las seis** instead. The time stays the same; the speaker group changes.

Compare three small families aloud: **pienso → pensamos**, **vuelvo → volvemos**, **pido → pedimos**. Now add a negative, a time and a follow-up: **No volvemos hoy. Volvemos mañana. ¿A qué hora sales tú?** A useful paradigm is a tool for changing a message, not an isolated list. When you encounter a new verb, record its infinitive, yo form and nosotros form. Those three forms often expose both the change and its limit.

Regional recognition has the same purpose. A speaker might ask **¿A qué hora volvés?** with voseo. Understand the question without “correcting” the speaker into tú. Use your practiced variety consistently and notice your interlocutor’s forms.'''),
'c2-u02':('Who performs the action, and who experiences it?', '''In **Ana lava el coche**, the subject Ana washes something else. In **Ana se lava**, the subject also receives the action. In **Ana se lava las manos**, the body part appears with an article; the pronoun already identifies whose hands are involved. *Ana lava sus manos* is not a good mechanical replacement for the ordinary routine expression. Context can justify a possessive, but it is not the default pattern to memorize.

A two-verb phrase gives you two useful positions: **Me voy a acostar temprano** and **Voy a acostarme temprano**. The pronoun still refers to yo in both. You cannot attach **se** just because the dictionary prints **acostarse**: that **se** marks the dictionary form, not every speaker. Notice also **nos acostamos** versus **me acuesto**; choosing the pronoun does not eliminate the stem change.

Build a three-step account with **antes de**, **después de** and **luego**. After a preposition, use an infinitive: **Después de levantarme, desayuno**. Ask a partner one practical follow-up about time or sequence. This checks whether the routine is comprehensible beyond a memorized list.'''),
'c2-u03':('Keep the referent clear', '''Suppose a shop assistant asks **¿Necesita la bolsa?** A useful answer is **Sí, la necesito**, because **la** retrieves the already identified bag. Without that shared context, **La necesito** leaves the listener wondering what you need. Pronouns reduce repetition only after the reference is sufficiently clear. In a longer exchange, repeat a noun when the topic changes: **La bolsa está bien, pero necesito las entradas. ¿Las tiene?**

The direct object can be a person. **Veo a Ana → La veo** shows why the personal **a** does not automatically create an indirect object. Ask what role the person plays in the event, rather than classifying every phrase beginning with **a** identically. Some regions use **le** for a male human direct object in accepted contexts; recognize this variation while practicing the broadly Latin American **lo/la** distinction here.

With two verbs, compare **Lo quiero comprar** and **Quiero comprarlo**. With a single ordinary finite verb, use **Lo compro**, not *comprolo*. Before substituting a pronoun, identify its referent, gender and number, and then choose a permitted position.'''),
'c2-u04':('Separate the thing from the recipient', '''Start with **Entrego las llaves a Elena**. The thing delivered is **las llaves**; the recipient is **Elena**. Replace the thing alone: **Las entrego a Elena**. Replace or repeat the recipient with a pronoun: **Le entrego las llaves a Elena**. Replace both: **Se las entrego a Elena**. The final phrase can remain when it clarifies who receives them. **Se** does not tell you whether the recipient is singular or plural.

Now change only the recipient: **Te las entrego a ti**. Change only the thing: **Se lo entrego a Elena**, referring to **el documento**. This controlled contrast is more useful than memorizing *se lo* as an indivisible word. The indirect pronoun comes first; **le/les** becomes **se** before **lo/la/los/las**. Neither **me** nor **te** undergoes that replacement.

After a preposition, use **para mí**, **para ti**, **para ella**, **conmigo** and **contigo** where appropriate. **Para mí** and the indirect pronoun **me** are not interchangeable building blocks in every sentence. Explain the recipient and the object in plain words before choosing the construction.'''),
'c2-u05':('Make the comparison fair', '''A comparison needs a dimension and a reference point. **Este hotel es mejor** may invite **¿Mejor en qué sentido?** Add a reason: **Es más tranquilo que el otro, pero está más lejos del centro**. **Mejor** and **peor** are common quality comparisons; do not create *más mejor* as the ordinary equivalent of “better.” Age can use **mayor/menor**, while physical size normally calls for **más grande/más pequeño** in this context.

Quantity follows a different pattern from adjective comparison. **Tiene tantas habitaciones como el otro hotel** makes **tantas** agree with **habitaciones**. **Es tan cómodo como el otro** keeps **tan** unchanged before an adjective. Compare **Cuesta más que el otro** with **Cuesta más de cien dólares**: the first compares two items, while the second sets a numerical threshold.

In a practical choice, avoid inventing a universal winner. State the user’s priorities first, compare at least two relevant features, and acknowledge a drawback. **Para una visita corta elegiría…** will become available later; for now **Prefiero este porque…** is enough to make a reasoned choice.'''),
'c2-u06':('An event log becomes a story', '''A receipt might list **10:00 — pago; 10:15 — llamada; 11:00 — salida**. To report the day afterward, turn the labels into actions: **Pagué a las diez. Llamé a la oficina a las diez y cuarto. Salí a las once**. Add an object pronoun only when its reference is recoverable: **Compré las entradas y las imprimí**. The pronoun does not change the preterite ending.

Check three contrasts before submitting a written account: **compro/compró**, **termino/terminó**, and **llegue/llegué**. Written accents are part of the form, not optional decoration. An unfamiliar accent can change person, tense, or mood. For **-car, -gar, -zar** verbs, the yo spelling change preserves the intended consonant before **é**; the rest of the regular preterite does not inherit that spelling automatically.

For the midterm, first produce an independent account without the examples in view. Then annotate each repair with its cause: wrong person, incorrect ending, missing accent, or unclear reference. A corrected copy is useful evidence of revision, but keep the original so you can distinguish independent production from supported editing.'''),
'c2-u07':('Add the third-person changes that strong stems do not cover', '''Not every irregular preterite uses a strong stem such as **tuv-**. Some **-ir** verbs change the stem vowel only in third-person forms: **pedí, pediste, pidió, pedimos, pedisteis, pidieron**; **dormí, dormiste, durmió, dormimos, dormisteis, durmieron**. Compare **Pidieron ayuda** with **Pedimos ayuda**. These are not the full present-tense stem-change patterns carried into the past.

Some verbs also change **i** to **y** between vowels in third-person preterites: **leyó, leyeron**, alongside **leí, leíste, leímos**. Learn **leyó el aviso** as a phrase and compare it with **leí el aviso**. Keep an irregular reference available during homework; independent assessment is a later check of what you can retrieve without it.

Meaning deserves equal attention. **Quise hablar con ella** presents a bounded desire or attempt in context; **no quise** often means refusing. It is too crude to replace every occurrence of querer with “try” solely because it is preterite. Ask what happened before and afterward, then choose a translation that fits the actual event.'''),
'c2-u08':('Background has a point of view', '''Consider **A las ocho, la plaza estaba vacía y llovía**. The speaker places the listener inside a past scene. The imperfect does not assert that the plaza remained empty forever or that the rain never stopped; those endpoints simply are not the focus. **Era pequeña** can describe a shop that still exists. “Temporary versus permanent” is not a reliable way to choose a past tense.

For habits, frequency expressions help establish a repeated scene: **Cada sábado íbamos al mercado**. A bounded summary can instead package many repetitions as one period: **Durante ese mes fuimos al mercado cuatro veces**. The number of real-world actions does not by itself force the imperfect. What matters is how the speaker presents them.

Write a short childhood scene using one location, one recurring action and one description. Then add a new sentence about a single event from that setting, reserving fuller contrasts for the next unit. A partner should be able to tell which information sets the scene and which event moves the account forward.'''),
'c2-u09':('Read the same event through two lenses', '''Compare **Cuando llegué, Ana preparaba café** with **Cuando llegué, Ana preparó café**. The first normally presents preparation as already in progress at the moment of arrival. The second presents the arrival and preparation as events in the sequence; it can suggest that she made coffee afterward. Do not choose a tense merely because **cuando** appears. Decide what relationship you want to communicate.

The same distinction affects summaries. **La reunión era larga** describes its character from within the situation. **La reunión fue larga** evaluates the meeting as a completed whole. Both can be grammatical descriptions of a real meeting; their viewpoints differ. A quiz must supply enough context to make the requested viewpoint clear. In your own writing, you can create that context.

Revise a story by underlining background in one style and bounded events in another. If every sentence is background, the listener may wait for something to happen. If every sentence advances the plot, the listener may lack a reason or setting. Good narration uses both to make the event understandable.'''),
'c2-u10':('A usable instruction includes a sequence and a check', '''Imagine helping someone print a ticket. **Abra el archivo. Compruebe la fecha. Imprímalo** is a coherent usted sequence. **No lo imprima todavía** changes both the polarity and the pronoun position. The command verb still refers to usted, while **lo** refers to the ticket or file. To address several people, use **abran, comprueben, imprímanlo**.

A more collaborative opening is **¿Puede abrir el archivo, por favor?** Follow with a check such as **¿Se ve la fecha?** Commands are one linguistic tool; they are not the only socially appropriate way to accomplish an instruction. A novice learner should practice asking for clarification as well: **¿Cuál archivo? ¿Así está bien?**

Pay attention to short irregular negative commands: **no vayas**, **no seas**, **no des**, alongside **ve**, **sé**, **da** in affirmative tú. Do not invent *no ve* by putting no before an affirmative command. Give a willing partner a harmless three-step task, let them introduce a problem, and adapt the instruction to their actual question.'''),
'c2-u11':('Connect a perfect form to the time frame', '''The present perfect combines present **haber** with a participle: **he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han** + **hablado/comido/vivido**. Frequent irregular participles include **hecho, dicho, visto, escrito, abierto, puesto, vuelto, roto**. With haber, the participle does not agree with the object: **He escrito las cartas**, not *he escritas*. With estar as a description, agreement is possible: **Las cartas están escritas**.

Some speakers commonly use **Hoy he hablado con Ana**, while others naturally say **Hoy hablé con Ana**. Treat this as a regional and contextual distribution, not proof that one speaker has failed to learn the tense. The present perfect can connect experience or a result to the present; it does not map mechanically to every English present perfect.

For **por/para**, group reasons by message: **por el parque** can describe a route, **por diez dólares** an exchange, **para Ana** an intended recipient, **para mañana** a deadline. Explain the relationship rather than translating the isolated English word “for.” An ambiguous sentence may allow both prepositions with different meanings.'''),
'c2-u12':('Turn planning notes into a message someone can use', '''A travel project should do more than display tense endings. Give the reader enough information to act: destination, sequence, practical constraints and a clear request. Then narrate a short completed episode with a setting and a result. Separate your future plan from your past experience so the listener can tell which details are confirmed, intended or already completed.

For example, **Vamos a salir a las ocho** describes a plan; **Salimos a las ocho ayer** reports an event; **Antes salíamos más tarde** describes an earlier habit. The identical-looking **salimos** needs context. Use pronouns when references are clear, but repeat a noun if two tickets or two people could be confused.

Prepare the presentation once, then ask a partner to change a constraint: the shop is closed, the train is delayed, or a traveler cannot eat an ingredient. Your response is a separate sample of communication. Record which part was prepared and which was spontaneous. Review a weak feature with a fresh scenario instead of retaking the same multiple-choice bank until its answers are memorized.'''),
'c3-u07':('Stem changes and spelling preserve more than a rule', '''With **-ar/-er** stem-changing verbs, nosotros and vosotros usually lack the present stem change: **piense/pensemos**, **vuelva/volvamos**. With many **-ir** verbs, a change also appears there: **pida/pidamos**, **duerma/durmamos**, **sienta/sintamos**. The smaller nosotros vowel change in **durmamos** and **sintamos** is not **ue/ie**. Build examples such as **Espero que durmamos bien** rather than guessing from the indicative **dormimos**.

Spelling also protects pronunciation: **buscar → busque**, **llegar → llegue**, **empezar → empiece/empecemos**. A complete decision has two stages. First choose mood from the construction and meaning. Then form the verb for the subject and spelling pattern. A perfectly spelled indicative can still express the wrong stance, while a correct subjunctive intention can still contain an agreement error.

Test both stages with a contrast: **Sé que vuelve** versus **Espero que vuelva**. Change the subject to nosotros: **Sé que volvemos** versus **Espero que volvamos**. Explain what the speaker asserts or hopes before labeling the endings.'''),
'c3-u05':('Which relative expression fits the relationship?', '''For a basic identifying clause without a preposition, **que** is often the flexible choice: **La persona que llamó** and **El libro que compré**. After a preposition referring to a person, several constructions can be legitimate: **la persona con quien hablé**, **con la que hablé**, or **con la cual hablé**. They differ in frequency and style but share the relevant relationship. A controlled exercise should accept those alternatives unless it asks for a particular construction.

Place can similarly use **donde**, **en que** or **en el/la que** when the surrounding noun and preposition fit: **la sala donde trabajamos / en la que trabajamos**. Do not substitute donde for every relationship; time, reason and possession need their own constructions. At this stage, choose a construction you can use accurately and recognize alternatives in reading.

Punctuation changes interpretation: **Los estudiantes que terminaron salieron** identifies a subset. **Los estudiantes, que terminaron, salieron** adds information about the identified group. Read each aloud and explain which students the speaker means before editing commas mechanically.'''),
'c4-u04':('Past and present consequences need different results', '''A counterfactual past condition typically uses **si hubiera/hubiese + participle**: **Si hubiera revisado la fecha, no habría perdido la cita**. The condition and missed appointment are both situated in the past. A different result can connect that past to now: **Si hubiera aceptado el puesto, ahora viviría en otra ciudad**. Here **viviría** describes a hypothetical present consequence, so a past-perfect result is not required.

The two imperfect-subjunctive series are both available in the condition. In many standard counterfactual results, **hubiera + participle** can also replace **habría + participle**: **Si lo hubiera sabido, te lo hubiera dicho**. Do not mark this common construction wrong simply because an introductory template teaches conditional perfect in the result. Conversely, do not generalize that interchangeability to every context containing hubiera or habría.

When correcting a hypothetical statement, draw a short timeline: when is the imagined condition, when is the consequence, and what does the speaker believe actually happened? Then choose the construction. A meaningful explanation includes that timeline, not only the names of two tenses.'''),
'c5-u04':('Una síntesis trabajada, con límites visibles', '''Supón tres fichas ficticias: A registra 240 **visitas** en cuatro tardes; B recoge entrevistas con seis personas que tuvieron dificultades para reservar; C calcula el costo de mantener abierto un turno adicional. No puedes escribir que “240 personas piden una ampliación”: A no cuenta necesariamente personas distintas y no mide una petición. Tampoco puedes convertir las seis entrevistas en un porcentaje de toda la población sin una muestra adecuada.

Una síntesis proporcional sería: **El registro muestra uso del servicio durante el período observado; las entrevistas identifican barreras que ese recuento no describe. La estimación de costos permite planificar una prueba, pero no demuestra por sí sola su beneficio.** Cada oración conserva la función de su fuente. La recomendación puede ser propia: **Proponemos una prueba limitada que mida usuarios distintos, intentos fallidos de reserva y costo por turno**.

Observa la diferencia entre resumir tres documentos por separado y relacionarlos mediante una pregunta. La síntesis explica qué aporta cada fuente, dónde no son comparables y qué decisión puede sostenerse. Identifica expresamente que estas cifras son un ejercicio ficticio, no datos de una biblioteca real.'''),
}
for uid,(title,body) in passages.items():
 p=ROOT/f'content/units/{uid}.json';u=json.loads(p.read_text());u['lectures']=[l for l in u['lectures'] if l['title']!=title]+[{'title':title,'body':body}];p.write_text(json.dumps(u,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(f'Applied {len(passages)} substantive teaching supplements.')
